.SH NAME
col \- nroff post-processing filter for printer output
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcol\fR [\fB\-bfx\fR] [ < \fIfile\fR] 
.br
\fBcat\fI file\fB | col\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
This filter removes halfline and reverse-line 
motions generated by \fBnroff\fR(1).
.PP
To display equations, tables, or multi-column
formats on a device that lacks reverse-line capability,
such as a line printer or video terminal,
run
.B nroff
without the -T option and filter the output with
.B col.
.LP
.B Col
is also used to optimize output to a TTY37.
The -f option should be used in this case.
.LP
These are
.BR col 's
primary features:
.IP \(bu
.B Col 
transforms input containing the TTY37 sequences
for reverse-line (ESC-7), reverse-halfline (ESC-8),
and forward-halfline (ESC-9) to a form suitable
for a device without these capabilities.
It does this by overlaying the text
lines on an internal buffer the same
way the TTY37 overlays the physical lines
on the output paper.
VT (^K)
is also assumed to mean reverse-line.
.IP \(bu
Where possible,
.B col
changes blanks to equivalent tabs.
Tab stops are assumed for the ninth columns
and every 8 columns thereafter.
.IP \(bu
The TTY37 uses
SI (^N) and SO (^O)
to shift to and from its
Greek character set.
.B Nroff
generates a SI and a SO for
.I every
Greek letter.
.B Col
eliminates the unnecessary shift-out-shift-in sequences.
.B Col
can be used to optimize any use of SI and SO
to indicate an alternate character set.
.IP \(bu
.B Col
eliminates all control characters except for
ESC (escape, hexadecimal 1b)
when followed by a 7, 8, or 9 character,
.RS 15
SP (space, 20),
.br
BS (backspace, 08),
.br
HT (tab, 09),
.br
CR (return, 0d),
.br
NL (newline, 0a),
.br
SI (shift in, 0f),
.br
SO (shift out, 0e),
.RE
.sp
.SH OPTIONS
.IP -b
Generate output suitable for a device that cannot backspace.
In a series of overstruck characters, only the last is output.
.IP -f
Eliminate all reverse motion but permit halfline-forward
(ESC-9) sequences.
Useful to optimize output to TTY37.
.IP -x
Do not generate new tab characters.
.SH SEE ALSO
troff(1), tbl(1), eqn(1), ascii(7)
.SH LIMITATIONS
Can't back up more than 128 lines.
.LP
Permits no more than 800 characters, including backspaces, on a line.
.LP
